+.. _python_api_transform_schema:
+
+Coordinated schema plans
+------------------------
+
+``db.transform_schema(plan, dry_run=False)`` applies a mapping of existing table
+names to changes in one transaction. It preserves incoming foreign keys, views,
+triggers and indexes, including partial and expression indexes::
+
+    plan = {
+        "products": {
+            "rename": {"name": "title"},
+            "types": {"price": "REAL"},
+            "defaults": {"price": 0.0},
+            "not_null": {"name": True},
+            "drop": ["obsolete"],
+            "column_order": ["id", "name", "price"],
+        },
+        "sales": {"types": {"quantity": int}, "strict": True},
+    }
+    preview = db.transform_schema(plan, dry_run=True)
+    report = db.transform_schema(plan)
+
+All column references use original names, resolved case-insensitively using
+SQLite rules. Renames may swap names. ``column_order`` may be partial. Omitted
+settings are preserved. Unknown options, duplicate references, conflicting
+changes and final-name collisions raise ``TransformError``. Primary keys may
+be renamed but their type and nullability cannot change. SQLite must accept
+all drops with dependent objects present. Native renames update dependencies
+while preserving explicit view output aliases and SQL literals.
+
+``types`` accepts INTEGER/int, REAL/float, TEXT/str and BLOB/bytes. Numeric
+conversions accept finite numbers and decimal numeric strings, with blank text
+becoming null. INTEGER additionally requires an integral signed 64-bit value.
+Numeric conversion rejects blobs. TEXT decodes blobs as strict UTF-8 and uses
+SQLite's text cast for numbers. BLOB encodes text as UTF-8 and rejects numbers.
+Null remains null. Invalid conversion aborts the whole plan. Existing rows and
+hidden rowids are preserved; no conflict policy may silently discard rows.
+
+``not_null`` maps columns to booleans. ``defaults`` accepts literal strings,
+signed-64-bit integers, finite floats and booleans; null removes a default. Strings such as
+``CURRENT_TIMESTAMP`` are literal strings, not SQL expressions. Existing SQL
+defaults are preserved when unmentioned. Defaults apply to future inserts and
+do not fill nulls. ``strict`` accepts a boolean and defaults to preserving the
+current table setting.
+
+This operation requires a connection outside a transaction, including for dry
+runs. It supports main-schema ordinary rowid tables and built-in SQL functions
+and collations. Selected virtual tables, generated-column tables, WITHOUT
+ROWID tables and tables shadowing all hidden rowid aliases are rejected. TEMP
+objects and attached databases are outside its scope. Ambiguous dependencies
+that native SQLite cannot rename or drop are rejected. Existing invalid foreign
+keys are rejected. All failures leave the original database unchanged.
+
+Execution acquires a write transaction before schema inspection. Rebuilds do
+not fire application triggers or cascade-delete children. CHECK/UNIQUE,
+collations, conflict policies, FK actions and deferrability, AUTOINCREMENT
+high-water marks and dependency names survive. Connection foreign_keys,
+defer_foreign_keys and legacy_alter_table settings are restored.
+
+Dry runs validate data and dependencies on an isolated snapshot, support a
+read-only source connection and leave its file bytes unchanged. Both modes
+return the same JSON-compatible report on unchanged input: ``tables`` lists
+planned tables by name, each with ``name``, ``rows`` and ordered ``columns``;
+``schema`` lists all main objects with non-null SQL except sqlite_-prefixed
+objects, sorted by type/name, each with ``type``, ``name``, ``table`` and ``sql``.
+The report contains final definitions without temporary rebuild names.
+
